---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_URL, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../lib/site';
import { CASE_QUOTES as Q, CASE_LINKS, QUESTIONS } from '../../data/grievanceFile';

const reviewHref = 'https://frazerappliedentomology.com/contact/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Vermin Grievance File: Eight Questions for a Pest Grievance"
  description="A printable worksheet for jails and prisons: the eight questions plaintiff's counsel asks about a vermin grievance, each paired with the record that answers it, built on the deliberate-indifference test in Farmer v. Brennan and Antonelli v. Sheahan."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Tools', url: `${SITE_URL}/tools/` }, { name: 'Vermin Grievance File', url: `${SITE_URL}/tools/vermin-grievance-file/` }]}
>
  <style is:global>
    .lede { font-size: 1.05rem; max-width: 46rem; }
    .notice { border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent); padding: 0.75rem 1rem; background: rgba(58, 90, 120, 0.06); font-size: 0.9rem; max-width: 46rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 1.75rem; }
    .case { border: 1px solid var(--color-rule); border-radius: 6px; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 1.75rem; max-width: 46rem; }
    .case h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.1rem; }
    .case p { margin: 0.5rem 0; }
    .dl { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.75rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 2rem; }
    .btn { display: inline-block; padding: 0.55rem 1rem; border-radius: 4px; border: 1px solid var(--color-primary); background: var(--color-primary); color: #fff; font: inherit; font-size: 0.92rem; cursor: pointer; text-decoration: none; }
    .btn.secondary { background: transparent; color: var(--color-primary); }
    .fields { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr)); gap: 0.6rem 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; font-size: 0.92rem; }
    .fields span { display: block; border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-top: 1.1rem; }
    table.gq { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0.5rem 0 1.5rem; }
    table.gq th, table.gq td { border: 1px solid var(--color-rule); padding: 0.55rem 0.55rem; vertical-align: top; text-align: left; }
    table.gq thead th { font-size: 0.76rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; color: var(--color-text-meta); background: rgba(31, 45, 58, 0.04); }
    td.n { font-weight: 700; width: 2rem; text-align: center; }
    .half { display: block; font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-text-meta); margin-bottom: 0.2rem; }
    .auth { display: block; font-size: 0.8rem; margin-top: 0.3rem; }
    td.mark { width: 7.5rem; font-size: 0.8rem; white-space: nowrap; }
    td.where { width: 9rem; }
    .close { max-width: 46rem; font-weight: 500; }
    .print-only { display: none; }
    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      table.gq thead { display: none; }
      table.gq tr { display: block; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-rule); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
      table.gq td { display: block; border: 0; padding: 0.25rem 0; width: auto !important; text-align: left !important; }
      td.mark, td.where { display: none !important; }
    }
    @media print {
      @page { size: letter portrait; margin: 0.5in; }
      html { font-size: 10.5px !important; }
      body { background: #fff !important; }
      main { max-width: none !important; margin: 0 !important; padding: 0 !important; }
      main h1 { font-size: 2.2rem; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
      .site-header, .site-footer, nav, .breadcrumbs, .no-print, .dl { display: none !important; }
      .print-only { display: block; font-size: 0.85rem; color: #555; margin-top: 0.6rem; }
      .lede, .notice, .close { max-width: none; }
      .case { max-width: none; padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem; margin: 0.8rem 0; }
      .worksheet { page-break-before: always; }
      table.gq { font-size: 0.95rem; }
      table.gq th, table.gq td { padding: 0.45rem 0.5rem; }
      table.gq tr { page-break-inside: avoid; }
      td.where { width: 12rem; }
      a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
    }
  </style>

  <h1>The Vermin Grievance File</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>Eight questions plaintiff's counsel asks about a pest grievance, and the record that answers each one.</strong>
    When a person held in a jail or prison claims that cockroaches, mice, bed bugs, or lice violated the Constitution, the
    claim turns on deliberate indifference. A prison official is liable under the Eighth Amendment only if the official "{Q.farmerKnows.text}"
    ({Q.farmerKnows.cite}). And officials who knew "{Q.farmerReasonable.text}" ({Q.farmerReasonable.cite}).
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Both halves are proved with paper. Knowledge is usually proved with the facility's own grievance, kite, or sick-call record.
    A reasonable response has to be proved with records too: the work order, the service record, the follow-up, and the
    answer to the grievance. The eight questions below walk one grievance from the first half to the second.
  </p>

  <div class="dl no-print">
    <button type="button" class="btn" onclick="window.print()">Print or save as PDF</button>
    <a class="btn secondary" href="/downloads/Vermin_Grievance_File_Eight_Questions.pdf" download>Download PDF</a>
    <a class="btn secondary" href="/tools/grievance-loop/">Grievance-to-Work-Order Loop</a>
  </div>

  <section class="case">
    <h2>Spraying is not the same as responding</h2>
    <p>
      In <a href={CASE_LINKS.antonelli}>Antonelli v. Sheahan</a>, the plaintiff alleged cockroaches and mice
      "{Q.antonelliJail.text}." The district court dismissed the claim, concluding that his allegation that the jail was
      "{Q.antonelliSprayed.text}." The Seventh Circuit reversed: "{Q.antonelliInsufficient.text}." The claim,
      the court wrote, "{Q.antonelliEither.text}." The defendants included the Sheriff, who, the court said,
      "{Q.antonelliSystemic.text}."
    </p>
    <p>
      In November 2024 the U.S. Department of Justice announced findings that "{Q.dojFulton.text}"
      (<a href={CASE_LINKS.doj}>{Q.dojFulton.cite}</a>).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="case">
    <h2>Pretrial detainees in the Second Circuit: what the official should have known</h2>
    <p>
      Most people held in a county jail have not been convicted. In the Second Circuit (New York, Connecticut, and Vermont), a
      pretrial detainee's conditions claim arises under the Fourteenth Amendment, and the mental element is measured objectively:
      the official is liable for acting intentionally, or recklessly failing to act with reasonable care, even though the official
      "{Q.darnellShould.text}" (<a href={CASE_LINKS.darnell}>Darnell v. Pineiro</a>, {Q.darnellShould.cite.replace('Darnell v. Pineiro, ', '')}).
      Negligence is not enough: "{Q.darnellNegligent.text}" ({Q.darnellNegligent.cite}). The plaintiffs in Darnell described
      holding cells "{Q.darnellVermin.text}" ({Q.darnellVermin.cite}).
    </p>
    <p>
      In <a href={CASE_LINKS.edwards}>Edwards v. Arocho</a>, a pretrial detainee's case from the Westchester County jail, the
      court concluded that allegations that he was "{Q.edwardsVermin.text}" ({Q.edwardsVermin.cite}). His complaints to
      "{Q.edwardsRounds.text}" were enough, at the pleading stage, to allege that the officers knew.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the file, the second half does not change: a dated work order, a service record for the location, a follow-up check,
      and an answer that matches them. Under a should-have-known standard, what the jail's own records show it was told carries
      more weight, not less. Other circuits are not surveyed here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="notice">
    This is a records worksheet. It is not legal advice and not a finding about any facility. The questions describe what a
    file would need to show; which records a facility keeps, and who is responsible for them, is set by its own policies,
    contracts, and jurisdiction. A facility reviewing a grievance tied to a pending or expected claim should do it through counsel.
  </p>

  <div class="worksheet">
  <div class="fields">
    <div>Facility<span></span></div>
    <div>Grievance or kite no.<span></span></div>
    <div>Date received<span></span></div>
    <div>Reviewed by / date<span></span></div>
  </div>

  <table class="gq">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">#</th>
        <th scope="col">Question</th>
        <th scope="col">Record that answers it</th>
        <th scope="col">On file?</th>
        <th scope="col">Where it is filed</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {QUESTIONS.map((q) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="n">{q.n}</td>
          <td><span class="half">{q.half}</span>{q.question}</td>
          <td>
            {q.record}
            {q.authority && <a class="auth" href={q.authority.href}>{q.authority.label}</a>}
          </td>
          <td class="mark">&#9744; Yes &nbsp;&#9744; No<br />&#9744; Partial</td>
          <td class="where"></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="close">
    Each question maps to a record. When every answer has a record behind it, the file shows what the facility did, when,
    and whether it worked.
  </p>
  </div>

  <p class="print-only">
    Corrections Pest Reference · correctionspestreference.org/tools/vermin-grievance-file/ · Trenton S. Frazer, M.S., BCE #B3413
  </p>

  <h2 class="no-print">Keep the loop closed</h2>
  <p class="no-print">
    The <a href="/tools/grievance-loop/">Grievance-to-Work-Order Loop</a> is a one-page log that ties each grievance to its work
    order, response, follow-up, and answer, so these eight answers exist before anyone asks. For the rules that set what a service
    record must contain, see the <a href="/tools/compliance-map/">Compliance Map</a>.
  </p>
  <h2 class="no-print">Have the file reviewed</h2>
  <p class="no-print">
    A Board Certified Entomologist can trace a facility's recent pest grievances through these eight questions and report where
    the record goes missing. See the <a href="/consulting/#vermin-grievance-file-review">Vermin Grievance File Review</a>, or
    <a href={reviewHref}>request one through the contact form</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
